---
import PluginLayout from '../../layouts/PluginLayout.astro';
import pluginsData from '../../data/plugins.json';
// Colors: orange #d4600a (accent), amber #f07820 (accentLight)
const p = pluginsData.find(x => x.name === 'Cascade Combat System')!;
---
<PluginLayout
  title="Cascade Combat System — Branching Combo Trees Built on GAS for UE5"
  description="A node-based combat framework for Unreal Engine 5. Build branching combo sequences with a visual graph editor, run damage-agnostic hit detection, and ship multiplayer-ready combat without writing replication code."
  pluginName="Cascade Combat System"
  version={p.version}
  coverImage={p.cover}
  logoImage={p.logo}
  fabUrl={p.fabUrl ?? '#'}
  accent={p.accent}
  accentLight={p.accentLight}
  bg="#090808"
  surface="#100c08"
  surface2="#180f08"
  codeBg="#090608"
>

  <!-- ── Sidebar nav ─────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <nav slot="sidebar-nav">
    <div class="nav-group">
      <span class="nav-group-label">Introduction</span>
      <a href="#overview">Overview</a>
      <a href="#getting-started">Getting Started</a>
    </div>
    <div class="nav-group">
      <span class="nav-group-label">Combo System</span>
      <a href="#combo-definition">Combo Definition</a>
      <a href="#combo-nodes">Combo Nodes</a>
      <a href="#combo-ability">Combo Ability</a>
    </div>
    <div class="nav-group">
      <span class="nav-group-label">Abilities</span>
      <a href="#anim-ability">Animation Abilities</a>
      <a href="#attack-ability">Attack Ability</a>
    </div>
    <div class="nav-group">
      <span class="nav-group-label">Combat</span>
      <a href="#hit-detection">Hit Detection</a>
      <a href="#anim-notifies">Animation Notifies</a>
      <a href="#weapons">Weapons</a>
    </div>
    <div class="nav-group">
      <span class="nav-group-label">Systems</span>
      <a href="#targeting">Targeting</a>
      <a href="#projectiles">Projectiles</a>
      <a href="#reactions">Reactions</a>
    </div>
    <div class="nav-group">
      <span class="nav-group-label">Multiplayer</span>
      <a href="#multiplayer">Replication</a>
    </div>
  </nav>

  <!-- ── Overview ───────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="doc-section" id="overview" data-section="overview">
    <h2 class="doc-section-title">
      <span class="section-icon">
        <svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/><line x1="12" y1="8" x2="12" y2="12"/><line x1="12" y1="16" x2="12.01" y2="16"/></svg>
      </span>
      Overview
    </h2>
    <div class="section-divider"></div>
    <p class="doc-p">
      <strong>Cascade Combat System</strong> is a node-based combo and melee framework built on the Gameplay Ability System for Unreal Engine 5. It provides the structural pieces — combo trees, animation-driven abilities, swept hit detection, targeting, projectiles, and hit reactions — without making decisions that belong to your game: damage formulas, input mapping style, movement integration, and art direction are all left to the project.
    </p>
    <p class="doc-p">
      The central data primitive is <strong>UCascadeComboDefinition</strong>, a data asset whose node tree is authored in a visual graph editor and executed at runtime by <strong>UCascadeAbilityTask_RunComboTree</strong>. Each node activates a <strong>UGameplayAbility</strong>, enabling the full GAS toolset — gameplay tags, attribute checks, gameplay effects, and prediction — at every combo step. Hit detection is driven by animation notifies rather than ability code, so swapping weapons or animations does not require changing ability blueprints.
    </p>

    <div class="feature-grid">
      <div class="feature-card">
        <div class="feature-icon">
          <svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="22 12 18 12 15 21 9 3 6 12 2 12"/></svg>
        </div>
        <p class="feature-title">Visual Combo Editor</p>
        <p class="feature-desc">Author branching, looping, and weighted-random combo trees in a dedicated graph editor. The compiler resolves back-edges and validates all node connections before the asset is saved.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature-card">
        <div class="feature-icon">
          <svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M14.7 6.3a1 1 0 0 0 0 1.4l1.6 1.6a1 1 0 0 0 1.4 0l3.77-3.77a6 6 0 0 1-7.94 7.94l-6.91 6.91a2.12 2.12 0 0 1-3-3l6.91-6.91a6 6 0 0 1 7.94-7.94l-3.76 3.76z"/></svg>
        </div>
        <p class="feature-title">Input-Agnostic</p>
        <p class="feature-desc">All input flows through <code>FGameplayTag</code>-identified input contexts. Enhanced Input, custom input systems, and AI all speak the same tag language. No hard dependency on any specific input framework.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature-card">
        <div class="feature-icon">
          <svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/><path d="M12 8v4l3 3"/></svg>
        </div>
        <p class="feature-title">Damage-Agnostic</p>
        <p class="feature-desc"><code>FCascadeHitData</code> carries who hit whom and gameplay tags describing the hit — never a damage number. Your project reads the context and computes the result. Works with any attribute set.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature-card">
        <div class="feature-icon">
          <svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M17 21v-2a4 4 0 0 0-4-4H5a4 4 0 0 0-4 4v2"/><circle cx="9" cy="7" r="4"/><path d="M23 21v-2a4 4 0 0 0-3-3.87"/><path d="M16 3.13a4 4 0 0 1 0 7.75"/></svg>
        </div>
        <p class="feature-title">Multiplayer Ready</p>
        <p class="feature-desc">Combo state replicates via FastArray. Melee hit detection runs server-side. Hit reactions replicate via Multicast RPCs. No custom replication code required in your project.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature-card">
        <div class="feature-icon">
          <svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><rect x="3" y="3" width="18" height="18" rx="2"/><path d="M3 9h18M9 21V9"/></svg>
        </div>
        <p class="feature-title">Notify-Driven Detection</p>
        <p class="feature-desc">Damage windows, combo windows, I-frames, and gameplay events are all placed on the animation timeline as notify states. Animation and ability code remain independent — swap a montage without touching the ability.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature-card">
        <div class="feature-icon">
          <svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M12 22s8-4 8-10V5l-8-3-8 3v7c0 6 8 10 8 10z"/></svg>
        </div>
        <p class="feature-title">Input Buffering</p>
        <p class="feature-desc">Two buffer modes: <em>Single Input</em> (one pre-window press, consumed on open — Genshin-style) and <em>Full Combo Queue</em> (pre-queue the entire chain — Spider-Man-style). Configured per character on the ASC.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="callout callout-info">
      <span class="callout-icon">
        <svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/><line x1="12" y1="8" x2="12" y2="12"/><line x1="12" y1="16" x2="12.01" y2="16"/></svg>
      </span>
      <span>Cascade Combat System requires the <strong>Gameplay Ability System</strong> (GAS) plugin. Your character must have a <code>UCascadeAbilitySystemComponent</code> — or a subclass of it — and an ability actor info configured before combat initialization.</span>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Getting Started ────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="doc-section" id="getting-started" data-section="getting-started">
    <h2 class="doc-section-title">
      <span class="section-icon">
        <svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polygon points="5 3 19 12 5 21 5 3"/></svg>
      </span>
      Getting Started
    </h2>
    <div class="section-divider"></div>
    <p class="doc-p">A character with a working single-attack combo is up in five steps. The fastest path requires no C++ beyond the module dependency.</p>

    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step">
        <span class="step-num">1</span>
        <div class="step-body">
          <p class="step-title">Enable the plugin</p>
          <p class="step-desc">In the Unreal Editor, open <strong>Edit → Plugins</strong>, search for <em>CascadeCombatSystem</em>, and enable it. Restart the editor when prompted. Also enable <strong>GameplayAbilities</strong> if it is not already active.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <span class="step-num">2</span>
        <div class="step-body">
          <p class="step-title">Add the module dependency</p>
          <p class="step-desc">In your project's <strong>Build.cs</strong>, add <code>"CascadeCombatSystem"</code> and <code>"GameplayAbilities"</code> to <code>PublicDependencyModuleNames</code>.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <span class="step-num">3</span>
        <div class="step-body">
          <p class="step-title">Add UCascadeAbilitySystemComponent to your character</p>
          <p class="step-desc">Replace or subclass the standard <code>UAbilitySystemComponent</code> on your character with <strong>UCascadeAbilitySystemComponent</strong>. Initialize actor info in <code>PossessedBy</code> (server) and <code>OnRep_PlayerState</code> (client) as normal for GAS.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <span class="step-num">4</span>
        <div class="step-body">
          <p class="step-title">Create a UCascadeCombatSetup asset</p>
          <p class="step-desc">Right-click in the Content Browser and select <strong>Miscellaneous → Data Asset → UCascadeCombatSetup</strong>. Add your <code>UCascadeComboDefinition</code> assets to the <strong>Combos</strong> array and map each input tag to a <code>UInputAction</code> in <strong>InputEntries</strong>.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <span class="step-num">5</span>
        <div class="step-body">
          <p class="step-title">Initialize combat</p>
          <p class="step-desc">Call <code>GrantCombatAbilities(CombatSetup)</code> from <code>PossessedBy</code> on the server (grants abilities to AI and player alike). Call <code>InitializeCombat(CombatSetup, InputComponent)</code> from <code>SetupPlayerInputComponent</code> on the owning client (binds Enhanced Input). For AI, call <code>InitializeCombatForAI(CombatSetup)</code> from <code>PossessedBy</code> on the server instead.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="doc-subsection">
      <h3 class="doc-subsection-title">Minimal C++ initialization</h3>
      <div class="code-block">
        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-lang">C++</span></div>
        <pre><code><span class="code-comment">// AMyCharacter.cpp — PossessedBy (server)</span>
<span class="code-cmd">CascadeASC</span>->GrantCombatAbilities(<span class="code-val">CombatSetup</span>);

<span class="code-comment">// AMyCharacter.cpp — SetupPlayerInputComponent (owning client)</span>
<span class="code-cmd">CascadeASC</span>->InitializeCombat(
    <span class="code-val">CombatSetup</span>,
    Cast&lt;UEnhancedInputComponent&gt;(InputComponent)
);</code></pre>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Combo Definition ───────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="doc-section" id="combo-definition" data-section="combo-definition">
    <h2 class="doc-section-title">
      <span class="section-icon">
        <svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M14 2H6a2 2 0 0 0-2 2v16a2 2 0 0 0 2 2h12a2 2 0 0 0 2-2V8z"/><polyline points="14 2 14 8 20 8"/></svg>
      </span>
      Combo Definition
    </h2>
    <div class="section-divider"></div>
    <p class="doc-p">
      <strong>UCascadeComboDefinition</strong> is a <code>UDataAsset</code> that describes a complete combo tree — its tag identity, root node, timeout, and activation inputs. Open one in the Cascade Combo Editor to author the node graph visually. The compiler converts the graph into an instanced node object tree that <code>UCascadeAbilityTask_RunComboTree</code> traverses at runtime.
    </p>

    <table class="prop-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Property</th><th>Type</th><th>Description</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td class="prop-name">ComboTag</td>
          <td class="prop-type">FGameplayTag</td>
          <td class="prop-desc">Unique identifier for this combo. Used for routing input, querying active state, and replication.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="prop-name">ActivationInputTags</td>
          <td class="prop-type">TArray&lt;FGameplayTag&gt;</td>
          <td class="prop-desc">Input tags that start this combo from the root node. Any matching tag activates the combo.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="prop-name">RootNode</td>
          <td class="prop-type">UCascadeComboNode*</td>
          <td class="prop-desc">Entry point of the combo tree. Instanced — set by the graph compiler.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="prop-name">GlobalTimeout</td>
          <td class="prop-type">float</td>
          <td class="prop-desc">Seconds of input silence before the combo auto-resets. 0 disables the timeout.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="prop-name">Priority</td>
          <td class="prop-type">int32</td>
          <td class="prop-desc">When multiple registered combos share an input tag, higher priority is evaluated first.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="prop-name">RequiredTagsToStart</td>
          <td class="prop-type">FGameplayTagContainer</td>
          <td class="prop-desc">Tags that must be present on the ASC to start this combo.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="prop-name">BlockedTags</td>
          <td class="prop-type">FGameplayTagContainer</td>
          <td class="prop-desc">Tags that prevent this combo from starting.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="doc-subsection">
      <h3 class="doc-subsection-title">Runtime combo building</h3>
      <p class="doc-p" style="margin-bottom:12px">Combos can also be assembled at runtime without the editor using <code>UCascadeRuntimeComboBuilder</code> or the static factory on the definition itself:</p>
      <div class="code-block">
        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-lang">C++</span></div>
        <pre><code><span class="code-comment">// Create a definition from an existing root node at runtime</span>
UCascadeComboDefinition* Def = UCascadeComboDefinition::CreateDynamicCombo(
    <span class="code-val">this</span>,
    <span class="code-val">ComboTag</span>,
    <span class="code-val">RootNode</span>
);
<span class="code-cmd">CascadeASC</span>->RegisterCombo(Def);</code></pre>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Combo Nodes ────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="doc-section" id="combo-nodes" data-section="combo-nodes">
    <h2 class="doc-section-title">
      <span class="section-icon">
        <svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="5" r="3"/><circle cx="5" cy="19" r="3"/><circle cx="19" cy="19" r="3"/><line x1="12" y1="8" x2="5" y2="16"/><line x1="12" y1="8" x2="19" y2="16"/></svg>
      </span>
      Combo Nodes
    </h2>
    <div class="section-divider"></div>
    <p class="doc-p">
      All nodes derive from <strong>UCascadeComboNode</strong> and are evaluated in sequence by <code>UCascadeAbilityTask_RunComboTree</code>. Each node receives the current <code>FCascadeComboInputContext</code> and decides whether to advance, branch, loop, or end the combo. You can subclass <code>UCascadeComboNode</code> in Blueprint or C++ for project-specific routing logic.
    </p>

    <table class="prop-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Node Class</th><th>Description</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td class="prop-name">UCascadeComboNode_Ability</td>
          <td class="prop-desc">Activates a <code>UGameplayAbility</code> subclass at this step. Carries <strong>NextNodes</strong> for linear sequencing and a <strong>GracePeriod</strong> — the window after ability end during which the next input is still accepted before the combo resets.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="prop-name">UCascadeComboNode_Branch</td>
          <td class="prop-desc">Routes to different next nodes based on which input tag was pressed. Populate the <strong>InputBranches</strong> map (tag → next node) to create light/heavy/dodge divergence points without duplicating abilities.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="prop-name">UCascadeComboNode_Conditional</td>
          <td class="prop-desc">Checks <strong>RequiredTags</strong> and <strong>BlockedTags</strong> on the ASC before proceeding. Routes to <strong>OnSuccess</strong> or <strong>OnFailure</strong> depending on the result. Use for airborne-only finishers or locked-target specials.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="prop-name">UCascadeComboNode_Loop</td>
          <td class="prop-desc">Routes back to an earlier node in the tree, enabling repeating sequences. <strong>MaxIterations</strong> caps the repetition count (0 = unlimited). The target node is resolved at compile time from the graph back-edge.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="prop-name">UCascadeComboNode_Random</td>
          <td class="prop-desc">Picks one child node using weighted probability. Each entry in <strong>Children</strong> carries a relative <strong>Weight</strong> — equal weights give uniform distribution. Use for attack variation without explicit branching.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="callout callout-info">
      <span class="callout-icon">
        <svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/><line x1="12" y1="8" x2="12" y2="12"/><line x1="12" y1="16" x2="12.01" y2="16"/></svg>
      </span>
      <span>Branch and Conditional nodes are <em>routing-only</em> — they do not activate abilities themselves. The task resolves them transparently and activates the <code>UCascadeComboNode_Ability</code> they point to. The <strong>ResolvedNode</strong> field on <code>FCascadeComboStepResult</code> always holds the final ability node.</span>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Combo Ability ──────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="doc-section" id="combo-ability" data-section="combo-ability">
    <h2 class="doc-section-title">
      <span class="section-icon">
        <svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M13 2L3 14h9l-1 8 10-12h-9l1-8z"/></svg>
      </span>
      Combo Ability
    </h2>
    <div class="section-divider"></div>
    <p class="doc-p">
      <strong>UCascadeCombo</strong> is the GAS ability that owns a combo's execution lifetime. When the ASC routes a matching input tag to it, <code>ActivateAbility</code> spawns a <strong>UCascadeAbilityTask_RunComboTree</strong> and hands it the resolved root node, combo tag, and timeout. The task then drives the tree — activating step abilities, consuming windowed inputs, enforcing the timeout, and ending the combo ability when the tree finishes.
    </p>
    <p class="doc-p">
      Subclass <code>UCascadeCombo</code> in Blueprint to receive lifecycle callbacks. Point the optional <strong>ComboDefinition</strong> property at a <code>UCascadeComboDefinition</code> asset to let the asset drive all tree settings, or configure <strong>ComboTag</strong>, <strong>RootNode</strong>, and <strong>ActivationInputTags</strong> directly on the class defaults.
    </p>

    <div class="doc-subsection">
      <h3 class="doc-subsection-title">Blueprint lifecycle events</h3>
      <table class="prop-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Event</th><th>When it fires</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class="prop-name">OnComboStarted</td>
            <td class="prop-desc">Once when the task activates the first step.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="prop-name">OnComboEnded(bWasCancelled)</td>
            <td class="prop-desc">When the tree finishes naturally (last node) or is externally cancelled.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="prop-name">OnStepActivated(StepIndex, NodeTag)</td>
            <td class="prop-desc">Each time a new ability node is activated by the task.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="prop-name">OnInputReceived(InputContext)</td>
            <td class="prop-desc">Each time an input is forwarded to this combo — whether or not it advances the tree.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="doc-subsection">
      <h3 class="doc-subsection-title">ASC combo API</h3>
      <p class="doc-p" style="margin-bottom:12px">The ASC exposes a query and control surface for active combos. All functions are Blueprint-callable.</p>
      <table class="prop-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Function</th><th>Description</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class="prop-name">ProcessComboInput(InputContext)</td>
            <td class="prop-desc">Route a tagged input to the active combo task. Returns true if consumed.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="prop-name">IsComboActive(ComboTag)</td>
            <td class="prop-desc">Returns true while a combo task is running for the given tag.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="prop-name">GetActiveComboInstance(ComboTag, OutInstance)</td>
            <td class="prop-desc">Returns the current step index, current node, and input history for a running combo.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="prop-name">EndCombo(ComboTag)</td>
            <td class="prop-desc">Gracefully ends the combo after the current step finishes.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="prop-name">CancelCombo(ComboTag)</td>
            <td class="prop-desc">Aborts the running ability and removes the combo immediately, bypassing grace period.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="prop-name">SetAirborneState(bIsAirborne)</td>
            <td class="prop-desc">Applies or removes <code>Cascade.State.Airborne</code>. Drive from your movement system so Conditional nodes can gate aerial abilities.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Animation Abilities ────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="doc-section" id="anim-ability" data-section="anim-ability">
    <h2 class="doc-section-title">
      <span class="section-icon">
        <svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/><polyline points="12 6 12 12 16 14"/></svg>
      </span>
      Animation Abilities
    </h2>
    <div class="section-divider"></div>
    <p class="doc-p">
      <strong>UCascadeAnimAbility</strong> is the base class for any ability driven by an animation montage. It handles montage playback via <code>UAbilityTask_PlayMontageAndWait</code>, optional flying-mode root-motion (so Z motion applies in Walking/Falling CMC states), and a Mover 2.0 override point. The ability ends automatically when the montage completes or is interrupted. Subclass in Blueprint for each individual attack step.
    </p>

    <table class="prop-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Property</th><th>Type</th><th>Description</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td class="prop-name">Montage</td>
          <td class="prop-type">UAnimMontage*</td>
          <td class="prop-desc">Montage played on ability activation.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="prop-name">MontageSectionName</td>
          <td class="prop-type">FName</td>
          <td class="prop-desc">Optional montage section to jump to on play.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="prop-name">PlayRate</td>
          <td class="prop-type">float</td>
          <td class="prop-desc">Montage play rate multiplier.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="prop-name">bUseFlyingModeForRootMotion</td>
          <td class="prop-type">bool</td>
          <td class="prop-desc">Switches CMC to MOVE_Flying before the montage so full 3D root motion is applied. Restores to MOVE_Falling on end.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="prop-name">bBlockCancellationUntilWindow</td>
          <td class="prop-type">bool</td>
          <td class="prop-desc">Ability starts non-cancellable. Cancellation re-enables when <code>UCascadeAN_SendGameplayEvent</code> fires <code>Cascade.Event.Ability.RecoveryStarted</code>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="prop-name">bCancelOnMovementInput</td>
          <td class="prop-type">bool</td>
          <td class="prop-desc">Also cancel the ability when movement input exceeds <strong>MovementCancelThreshold</strong> once the cancellable window is open. Requires <code>bBlockCancellationUntilWindow</code>.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p class="doc-p">
      Override <code>PlayMontage</code> as a Blueprint Native Event to drive playback through a custom movement system (e.g. Mover 2.0) instead of the default CMC path.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Attack Ability ─────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="doc-section" id="attack-ability" data-section="attack-ability">
    <h2 class="doc-section-title">
      <span class="section-icon">
        <svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M18 8h1a4 4 0 0 1 0 8h-1"/><path d="M2 8h16v9a4 4 0 0 1-4 4H6a4 4 0 0 1-4-4V8z"/><line x1="6" y1="1" x2="6" y2="4"/><line x1="10" y1="1" x2="10" y2="4"/><line x1="14" y1="1" x2="14" y2="4"/></svg>
      </span>
      Attack Ability
    </h2>
    <div class="section-divider"></div>
    <p class="doc-p">
      <strong>UCascadeAttackAbility</strong> extends <code>UCascadeAnimAbility</code> with hit context and hit pause. Use it as the base class for any ability that produces melee or projectile hits. Hit detection itself is not in the ability — it lives on <code>UCascadeANS_DamageWindow</code> notifies in the montage. The ability contributes context that the notify merges into each <code>FCascadeHitData</code>.
    </p>

    <table class="prop-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Property</th><th>Type</th><th>Description</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td class="prop-name">HitTags</td>
          <td class="prop-type">FGameplayTagContainer</td>
          <td class="prop-desc">Tags merged into every <code>FCascadeHitData.HitTags</code> this ability produces. Use to mark hit strength (<code>Hit.Strength.Heavy</code>), damage type (<code>Damage.Type.Slash</code>), and similar semantic context your damage formula reads.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="prop-name">HitCustomData</td>
          <td class="prop-type">TMap&lt;FName, float&gt;</td>
          <td class="prop-desc">Open key-value bag merged into <code>FCascadeHitData.CustomData</code>. The plugin never reads this; your project uses it for numeric context (charge level, combo count, stance bonus).</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="prop-name">HitPauseDuration</td>
          <td class="prop-type">float</td>
          <td class="prop-desc">Seconds to freeze attacker and victim time dilation on hit. 0 disables.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="prop-name">HitPauseTimeDilation</td>
          <td class="prop-type">float</td>
          <td class="prop-desc">Time dilation multiplier applied during the hit pause window (0.01–0.99).</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="callout callout-info">
      <span class="callout-icon">
        <svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/><line x1="12" y1="8" x2="12" y2="12"/><line x1="12" y1="16" x2="12.01" y2="16"/></svg>
      </span>
      <span><strong>FCascadeHitData intentionally carries no damage value.</strong> Damage formulas are a game concern. Bind to <code>OnHitDetected</code> on the ASC, read the hit context (who, tags, custom data), and apply damage however your project requires.</span>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Hit Detection ──────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="doc-section" id="hit-detection" data-section="hit-detection">
    <h2 class="doc-section-title">
      <span class="section-icon">
        <svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M22 11.08V12a10 10 0 1 1-5.93-9.14"/><polyline points="22 4 12 14.01 9 11.01"/></svg>
      </span>
      Hit Detection
    </h2>
    <div class="section-divider"></div>
    <p class="doc-p">
      Melee hit detection runs inside <strong>UCascadeANS_DamageWindow</strong>, an anim notify state placed on the montage timeline. Each tick of the notify sweeps the configured socket segments against the collision channel, deduplicates hits per actor per window instance, and broadcasts <code>FCascadeHitData</code> on the attacker's ASC via <code>OnHitDetected</code>. A single actor can only be hit once per damage window per montage instance regardless of how many segments overlap it.
    </p>

    <div class="doc-subsection">
      <h3 class="doc-subsection-title">Trace configuration</h3>
      <p class="doc-p" style="margin-bottom:12px">
        <strong>FCascadeWeaponTraceConfig</strong> defines the sweep shape, collision channel, and socket segments for a weapon. It lives on <strong>ACascadeWeaponActor</strong>. When <code>bUseWeaponMesh</code> is enabled on the notify, the weapon's config is read automatically — no animation changes needed when swapping weapons. Disable <code>bUseWeaponMesh</code> and set <code>TraceConfig</code> directly on the notify for character-mesh traces (unarmed combat, tails, claws).
      </p>

      <table class="prop-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Property</th><th>Description</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class="prop-name">TraceSegments</td>
            <td class="prop-desc">Array of <code>FCascadeSocketSegment</code> entries — each maps a start socket to an end socket on the mesh. One segment per damage zone (blade, tip, handle).</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="prop-name">TraceShape</td>
            <td class="prop-desc"><code>ECascadeTraceShape</code>: Line, Box, Sphere, or Capsule.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="prop-name">TraceRadius / TraceHalfHeight / TraceBoxExtent</td>
            <td class="prop-desc">Shape dimensions — only the fields relevant to the selected shape are shown in the editor.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="prop-name">TraceChannel</td>
            <td class="prop-desc">Collision channel swept each tick. Set the weapon mesh profile to ignore this channel to exclude self-hits.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="doc-subsection">
      <h3 class="doc-subsection-title">Socket picker</h3>
      <p class="doc-p" style="margin-bottom:12px">
        Socket name fields use <strong>FCascadeSocketName</strong> — a metadata-tagged struct that shows a searchable picker in the editor, populated from the owning mesh's sockets and bones via the <code>ICascadeTraceConfigProvider</code> interface. Set sockets on the weapon mesh for weapon traces, or on the character skeleton for unarmed traces.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="doc-subsection">
      <h3 class="doc-subsection-title">Hit data</h3>
      <p class="doc-p" style="margin-bottom:12px">Every hit produces one <code>FCascadeHitData</code> broadcast on the attacker's ASC.</p>
      <table class="prop-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Field</th><th>Type</th><th>Description</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class="prop-name">Attacker</td>
            <td class="prop-type">AActor*</td>
            <td class="prop-desc">Actor that performed the attack.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="prop-name">Victim</td>
            <td class="prop-type">AActor*</td>
            <td class="prop-desc">Actor that was hit.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="prop-name">HitResult</td>
            <td class="prop-type">FHitResult</td>
            <td class="prop-desc">Exact trace result — location, normal, bone, component.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="prop-name">SourceAbility</td>
            <td class="prop-type">UGameplayAbility*</td>
            <td class="prop-desc">The ability that triggered this hit, or null.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="prop-name">HitTags</td>
            <td class="prop-type">FGameplayTagContainer</td>
            <td class="prop-desc">Merged tags from the notify and the ability (hit type, strength, zone).</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="prop-name">CustomData</td>
            <td class="prop-type">TMap&lt;FName, float&gt;</td>
            <td class="prop-desc">Project-specific numeric context merged from <code>UCascadeAttackAbility::HitCustomData</code>.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="doc-subsection">
      <h3 class="doc-subsection-title">Damage application pattern</h3>
      <div class="code-block">
        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-lang">C++</span></div>
        <pre><code><span class="code-comment">// Bind once in BeginPlay or PossessedBy</span>
<span class="code-cmd">CascadeASC</span>->OnHitDetected.AddDynamic(<span class="code-val">this</span>, &AMyCharacter::OnHitDetected);

void AMyCharacter::OnHitDetected(const FCascadeHitData& HitData)
&#123;
    <span class="code-comment">// Your project computes damage from the context</span>
    float Damage = ComputeDamage(HitData.HitTags, HitData.CustomData);
    <span class="code-cmd">ApplyDamageToVictim</span>(HitData.Victim, Damage, HitData.HitResult);
&#125;</code></pre>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Animation Notifies ─────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="doc-section" id="anim-notifies" data-section="anim-notifies">
    <h2 class="doc-section-title">
      <span class="section-icon">
        <svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M18 8A6 6 0 0 0 6 8c0 7-3 9-3 9h18s-3-2-3-9"/><path d="M13.73 21a2 2 0 0 1-3.46 0"/></svg>
      </span>
      Animation Notifies
    </h2>
    <div class="section-divider"></div>
    <p class="doc-p">
      All Cascade notifies are Branching Point notifies — they fire on the game thread at the exact animation frame, not between frames. Place them directly on the montage timeline in the Animation Editor. All notifies are per-montage-instance keyed, so concurrent plays on different characters do not interfere.
    </p>

    <table class="prop-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Notify</th><th>Type</th><th>Description</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td class="prop-name">Cascade: Damage Window</td>
          <td class="prop-type">Notify State</td>
          <td class="prop-desc"><code>UCascadeANS_DamageWindow</code> — continuous swept hit detection for the duration of the notify. Fires <code>OnHitDetected</code> for each new actor hit. Configure <code>HitTags</code> per-notify for per-swing semantic tags.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="prop-name">Cascade: Combo Window</td>
          <td class="prop-type">Notify State</td>
          <td class="prop-desc"><code>UCascadeANS_ComboWindow</code> — marks the input acceptance window. The task only advances the combo tree while this window is open. Drives input buffering — inputs that land outside the window are held and replayed when the next window opens.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="prop-name">Cascade: I-Frames</td>
          <td class="prop-type">Notify State</td>
          <td class="prop-desc"><code>UCascadeANS_IFrames</code> — applies <code>Cascade.State.Invulnerable</code> as a loose gameplay tag for the duration. Damage windows automatically skip actors carrying this tag. Enable <code>bApplyDodgingTag</code> for dodge rolls where both tags carry meaning.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="prop-name">Cascade: Send Gameplay Event</td>
          <td class="prop-type">Point Notify</td>
          <td class="prop-desc"><code>UCascadeAN_SendGameplayEvent</code> — sends a single gameplay event to the owner's ASC at a specific frame. Use to signal phase transitions: <code>Cascade.Event.Ability.RecoveryStarted</code> re-enables cancellation; <code>Cascade.Event.Combo.RequestEnd</code> gracefully ends a running combo.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="prop-name">Cascade: Spawn Projectile</td>
          <td class="prop-type">Point Notify</td>
          <td class="prop-desc"><code>UCascadeAN_SpawnProjectile</code> — spawns an <code>ACascadeProjectile</code> at a socket location on the weapon or character mesh. Inherits hit tags from the owning ability via the ASC.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Weapons ────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="doc-section" id="weapons" data-section="weapons">
    <h2 class="doc-section-title">
      <span class="section-icon">
        <svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><line x1="18" y1="6" x2="6" y2="18"/><line x1="6" y1="6" x2="18" y2="18"/></svg>
      </span>
      Weapons
    </h2>
    <div class="section-divider"></div>
    <p class="doc-p">
      <strong>ACascadeWeaponActor</strong> is a replicated actor that owns the hit detection configuration for a weapon and optionally grants combat abilities when drawn. It implements <code>ICascadeTraceConfigProvider</code> so damage window notifies can read the weapon's trace setup without coupling to a specific mesh type. Subclass in Blueprint — add your <code>USkeletalMeshComponent</code> or <code>UStaticMeshComponent</code> and override <code>GetWeaponComponent</code> to return it.
    </p>

    <table class="prop-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Property</th><th>Type</th><th>Description</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td class="prop-name">TraceConfig</td>
          <td class="prop-type">FCascadeWeaponTraceConfig</td>
          <td class="prop-desc">Sockets, trace shape, and collision channel for this weapon's hit detection. Read by <code>UCascadeANS_DamageWindow</code> when <code>bUseWeaponMesh</code> is true.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="prop-name">ProjectileSpawnSockets</td>
          <td class="prop-type">TArray&lt;FCascadeSocketName&gt;</td>
          <td class="prop-desc">Sockets on this weapon used as projectile spawn origins by <code>UCascadeAN_SpawnProjectile</code>. One projectile per socket entry.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="prop-name">DrawnSocket / SheathSocket</td>
          <td class="prop-type">FName</td>
          <td class="prop-desc">Character skeleton sockets the weapon re-attaches to on draw and sheath. Leave <code>NAME_None</code> for games without a socket system.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="prop-name">CombatSetup</td>
          <td class="prop-type">UCascadeCombatSetup*</td>
          <td class="prop-desc">Optional data asset — abilities and combos granted to the owner's ASC when the weapon is equipped. Gracefully skipped if the owner has no ASC.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="prop-name">EquipEffect</td>
          <td class="prop-type">TSubclassOf&lt;UGameplayEffect&gt;</td>
          <td class="prop-desc">Optional infinite-duration GameplayEffect applied on equip (passive stat bonuses). Removed automatically when the weapon actor is destroyed.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="doc-subsection">
      <h3 class="doc-subsection-title">Finding an equipped weapon at runtime</h3>
      <div class="code-block">
        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-lang">C++</span></div>
        <pre><code><span class="code-comment">// Get the currently drawn weapon attached to this actor</span>
ACascadeWeaponActor* Weapon = ACascadeWeaponActor::FindDrawnWeapon(OwnerActor);

<span class="code-comment">// Or get its mesh component directly (e.g. for socket queries)</span>
UMeshComponent* WeaponMesh = ACascadeWeaponActor::FindDrawnWeaponComponent(OwnerActor);</code></pre>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Targeting ──────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="doc-section" id="targeting" data-section="targeting">
    <h2 class="doc-section-title">
      <span class="section-icon">
        <svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="3"/><line x1="12" y1="2" x2="12" y2="5"/><line x1="12" y1="19" x2="12" y2="22"/><line x1="2" y1="12" x2="5" y2="12"/><line x1="19" y1="12" x2="22" y2="12"/></svg>
      </span>
      Targeting
    </h2>
    <div class="section-divider"></div>
    <p class="doc-p">
      <strong>UCascadeTargetingSubsystem</strong> is a world subsystem that selects the best combat target given a source actor and direction. It operates in two modes: <em>Unlocked</em> (scores all <code>ICascadeTargetable</code> actors in range and picks the best) and <em>Locked</em> (returns the locked target directly). Abilities and AI query the subsystem instead of doing their own overlap tests.
    </p>

    <div class="doc-subsection">
      <h3 class="doc-subsection-title">Finding a target</h3>
      <div class="code-block">
        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-lang">C++</span></div>
        <pre><code>UCascadeTargetingSubsystem* Targeting = GetWorld()->GetSubsystem&lt;UCascadeTargetingSubsystem&gt;();

FCascadeTargetingRequest Request;
Request.SourceActor = this;
Request.PreferredDirection = GetActorForwardVector();
<span class="code-comment">// Profile controls range, angle, weights, and LoS requirement</span>
Request.Profile.MaxRange = 800.f;
Request.Profile.MaxAngleDegrees = 45.f;
Request.Profile.bRequireLineOfSight = true;

FCascadeTargetingResult Result = Targeting->FindBestTarget(Request);</code></pre>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="doc-subsection">
      <h3 class="doc-subsection-title">Lock-on targeting</h3>
      <p class="doc-p" style="margin-bottom:12px">Switch the subsystem into Locked mode to make all subsequent targeting queries return the pinned target:</p>
      <div class="code-block">
        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-lang">C++</span></div>
        <pre><code><span class="code-comment">// Lock onto a specific target</span>
Targeting->SetTargetingMode(ECascadeTargetingMode::Locked, TargetActor);

<span class="code-comment">// Return to free targeting</span>
Targeting->SetTargetingMode(ECascadeTargetingMode::Unlocked, nullptr);</code></pre>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="doc-subsection">
      <h3 class="doc-subsection-title">Custom scoring</h3>
      <p class="doc-p" style="margin-bottom:12px">Subclass <strong>UCascadeTargetScoringPolicy</strong> in Blueprint and override <code>ScoreTarget</code> to implement project-specific priority rules (prefer wounded enemies, deprioritize allies, score by threat level). Assign the policy class in the request profile or the subsystem's default config.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="doc-subsection">
      <h3 class="doc-subsection-title">Reusable targeting profiles</h3>
      <p class="doc-p" style="margin-bottom:12px"><strong>UCascadeTargetingProfile</strong> is a data asset wrapping one <code>FCascadeTargetingProfileData</code>. Create named profiles (MeleeRange, BowRange, AOESearch) and pass them to <code>BuildRequestFromProfile</code> to avoid duplicating range and angle values across abilities.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Projectiles ────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="doc-section" id="projectiles" data-section="projectiles">
    <h2 class="doc-section-title">
      <span class="section-icon">
        <svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><line x1="5" y1="12" x2="19" y2="12"/><polyline points="12 5 19 12 12 19"/></svg>
      </span>
      Projectiles
    </h2>
    <div class="section-divider"></div>
    <p class="doc-p">
      <strong>ACascadeProjectile</strong> is a Blueprintable actor that travels through the world and broadcasts <code>OnHitDetected</code> on the instigator's ASC on impact, feeding directly into the same damage path as melee hits. Spawn it via the <strong>Cascade: Spawn Projectile</strong> point notify in a montage to keep projectile creation synchronized with animation frames.
    </p>

    <table class="prop-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Property</th><th>Description</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td class="prop-name">CollisionRadius</td>
          <td class="prop-desc">Radius of the sphere collision component in cm.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="prop-name">PierceCount</td>
          <td class="prop-desc">Additional actors the projectile can hit after the first before it is destroyed. 0 = single hit.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="prop-name">bDestroyOnEnvironmentHit</td>
          <td class="prop-desc">Destroy when hitting world geometry (actors with no ASC).</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="prop-name">Lifetime</td>
          <td class="prop-desc">Seconds before auto-destroy. 0 disables.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="prop-name">DefaultHitTags</td>
          <td class="prop-desc">Tags present on every hit from this projectile class. Merged with per-spawn tags from the ability.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="prop-name">MovementStrategyClass</td>
          <td class="prop-desc">Optional strategy. When set, <code>UProjectileMovementComponent</code> is disabled and this strategy drives movement each tick. Leave null for default straight-line movement.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="doc-subsection">
      <h3 class="doc-subsection-title">Movement strategies</h3>
      <p class="doc-p" style="margin-bottom:12px">Set <strong>MovementStrategyClass</strong> on your projectile Blueprint to override default straight movement. Three strategies ship with the plugin:</p>
      <table class="prop-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Strategy</th><th>Description</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class="prop-name">UCascadeProjectileMovementStrategy_Bezier</td>
            <td class="prop-desc">Cubic Bezier arc toward the target location. Configure <strong>TravelDuration</strong>, <strong>P1Offset</strong> (from spawn), and <strong>P2Offset</strong> (from destination) to shape the arc. Good for thrown objects, lobbed grenades, and stylized arrow trajectories.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="prop-name">UCascadeProjectileMovementStrategy_Homing</td>
            <td class="prop-desc">Continuous steering toward a target actor each tick. Works with any actor that implements <code>ICascadeTargetable</code>.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="prop-name">UCascadeProjectileMovementStrategy_Boomerang</td>
            <td class="prop-desc">Travels outward then curves back toward the instigator. Handles return-path hit detection separately so the same actor can be hit on the outbound and return legs.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Reactions ──────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="doc-section" id="reactions" data-section="reactions">
    <h2 class="doc-section-title">
      <span class="section-icon">
        <svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M21 15a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H7l-4 4V5a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h14a2 2 0 0 1 2 2z"/></svg>
      </span>
      Reactions
    </h2>
    <div class="section-divider"></div>
    <p class="doc-p">
      <strong>UCascadeReactionComponent</strong> is an optional actor component that translates damage events into data-driven hit and death reactions. Add it to any character that should play montages, VFX, or sounds in response to being hit. Assign a <strong>UCascadeReactionSetup</strong> data asset — one asset per enemy type — and the component handles everything from there.
    </p>
    <p class="doc-p">
      If the default handler model does not fit your needs, skip the component entirely and bind to <code>OnDamageTaken</code> on the ASC directly.
    </p>

    <div class="doc-subsection">
      <h3 class="doc-subsection-title">UCascadeReactionSetup</h3>
      <table class="prop-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Property</th><th>Description</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class="prop-name">HitHandlers</td>
            <td class="prop-desc">Instanced handler objects evaluated in descending <strong>Priority</strong> order. The first whose <code>CanHandle</code> returns true fires; the rest are skipped.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="prop-name">DeathHandlers</td>
            <td class="prop-desc">Same first-match-wins evaluation as HitHandlers, but no cooldown gate. Fires once when the ASC receives the death tag.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="prop-name">GlobalHitReactionCooldown</td>
            <td class="prop-desc">Minimum seconds between consecutive hit reactions. Prevents montage spam during rapid multi-hit attacks.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="prop-name">bRagdollOnDeath</td>
            <td class="prop-desc">Simulate physics on the character mesh after the death montage ends.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="prop-name">bDisableCollisionOnDeath</td>
            <td class="prop-desc">Disable actor collision when the owner dies.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="doc-subsection">
      <h3 class="doc-subsection-title">Reaction handlers</h3>
      <p class="doc-p" style="margin-bottom:12px">
        Subclass <strong>UCascadeReactionHandler</strong> in Blueprint or C++ to define a reaction. Override <code>CanHandle</code> to gate on hit tags (heavy hit, backstab, etc.) and <code>ExecuteReaction</code> to perform the response. Use <strong>UCascadeReactionHandler_Montage</strong> (included) for montage + VFX + sound without writing any code.
      </p>
      <p class="doc-p">
        Blueprint events on <code>UCascadeReactionComponent</code> fire after each handler executes: <code>OnHitReactionExecuted</code>, <code>OnDeathReactionExecuted</code>, and <code>OnNoReactionFound</code> (fired when all handlers are blocked by cooldown, active reaction, or tag mismatch).
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Multiplayer ────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="doc-section" id="multiplayer" data-section="multiplayer">
    <h2 class="doc-section-title">
      <span class="section-icon">
        <svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M17 21v-2a4 4 0 0 0-4-4H5a4 4 0 0 0-4 4v2"/><circle cx="9" cy="7" r="4"/><path d="M23 21v-2a4 4 0 0 0-3-3.87"/><path d="M16 3.13a4 4 0 0 1 0 7.75"/></svg>
      </span>
      Multiplayer
    </h2>
    <div class="section-divider"></div>
    <p class="doc-p">
      Cascade Combat System is server-authoritative. All hit detection, ability activation, and combo progression run on the server. Clients receive replicated state updates and play cosmetic feedback locally.
    </p>

    <div class="doc-subsection">
      <h3 class="doc-subsection-title">Combo state replication</h3>
      <p class="doc-p" style="margin-bottom:12px">
        Active combo instances replicate via <strong>FCascadeComboInstanceContainer</strong>, a <code>FFastArraySerializer</code> on the ASC. Each item carries the combo tag, current step index, and current node tag — enough for clients to display UI (combo step indicators, locked-target indicators) without running tree evaluation themselves. Delta compression keeps bandwidth minimal even with many simultaneous combos.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="doc-subsection">
      <h3 class="doc-subsection-title">Melee hit detection</h3>
      <p class="doc-p" style="margin-bottom:12px">
        <code>UCascadeANS_DamageWindow</code> runs its sweep traces on the server only. When a hit is detected, the server broadcasts <code>OnHitDetected</code> on the attacker's ASC. Your project applies the <code>UGameplayEffect</code> damage there — GAS handles prediction and client-side attribute updates as normal.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="doc-subsection">
      <h3 class="doc-subsection-title">Hit reactions</h3>
      <p class="doc-p" style="margin-bottom:12px">
        <code>UCascadeReactionComponent</code> listens to <code>OnDamageTaken</code> server-side, selects the handler, then calls <strong>Multicast_TriggerHitReaction</strong> and <strong>Multicast_TriggerDeathReaction</strong> to replicate the reaction to all clients. <code>FCascadeHitData</code> is not RPC-safe (it contains a <code>TMap</code>), so the component unpacks the relevant fields before the RPC.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="doc-subsection">
      <h3 class="doc-subsection-title">Weapon draw state</h3>
      <p class="doc-p" style="margin-bottom:12px">
        <code>ACascadeWeaponActor::bIsDrawn</code> replicates via <code>OnRep_IsDrawn</code>. All machines re-attach the weapon to <code>DrawnSocket</code> or <code>SheathSocket</code> on rep. Bind to <code>OnWeaponStateChanged</code> in Blueprint for animation, sound, or VFX on all clients.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="callout callout-info">
      <span class="callout-icon">
        <svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/><line x1="12" y1="8" x2="12" y2="12"/><line x1="12" y1="16" x2="12.01" y2="16"/></svg>
      </span>
      <span>Call <code>GrantCombatAbilities</code> from <code>PossessedBy</code> on the server so abilities are granted for remotely controlled pawns before the client's <code>InitializeCombat</code> runs. If abilities are granted after <code>InitializeCombat</code>, input bindings will be missing on the client until the next possession cycle.</span>
    </div>
  </section>

</PluginLayout>
